A||||7||RZURCTN||Fuel system problem, need help!!!.....||||5||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||01-03-2000||10:17 PM||||I put the engine back in my Fiero today, now the TBI isn't metering out anything, the fuel pump works fine, and I'm getting spark.  The distributor is good.  I can get the car to run on ether (starting fluid), but the TBI just won't pump out the fuel.  AND, the check engine light doesn't come on AT ALL anymore. If anyone has any insight into my problem, please help me out.  The Haynes manual just isn't any help.<BR>   All the stuff on  the engine now was just on another engine last week and it all worked fine, it's just that the other engine had too much blow-by in the #2 cyl. so I got a different one.  One big question, could it be the ECM?  I have another one I could use, but was wondering if I should give it a try.  Thanks for all you help!! <BR>                 -RZURCTN ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||01-04-2000||12:04 AM||||You should have at least one ECM fuse. Maybe two. Seems like one of those is also for the injector circuit. (Going from memory, here. Could be wrong.) I'm guessing that something accidentally got shorted during the engine work, and popped one of those fuses.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||geyser||01-04-2000||01:13 AM||||Make sure ALL your intake and smog connections are made, including the air cleaner and intake in its proper place.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||01-04-2000||03:28 AM||||I'm not sure what year yours is, but on my 84 there is a single wire connector in the harness located on the back of the engine between the distributor and the oil pressure switch.  This is the main power to the ecm and if it is not securely connected the ecm will be dead.  The ignition will still work, but no fuel injection or check engine light. Unhooking this connector will clear the ecm memory without disconnecting the battery and losing all your radio station presets.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Smokey||01-04-2000||09:58 PM||||I had the same problem. I was going down the road one day and she just quit getting gas. First I unhooked my fuel lines and checked to see if the pump was working...spit gas everywhere. Checked the fuel filter next...just dandy. then i started ripping apart my throttle body....it was getting gas all the way up to the injector. so i went and got a new injector...figured it was clogged....didn't work. then i checked the two little wires that plugged into the injector and found that they landed on the block and got burned up. repaired all the wiring and she fired right up!||||reg||1
Z||000005||RZURCTN||01-04-2000||10:12 PM||||Thanks for the help folks!  It turns out that I fried the ECM.  Luckily I had a spare on hand and replaced it, now it works fine.  Once I took a part the old one, I found where a connection had burned, it is repairable, so I'll still have a spare.  Once again, thanks for your help!||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||01-05-2000||02:53 PM||||Just curious... and so I don't do it myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ... <P>How did you fry the ECM???||||reg||1
Z||000007||RZURCTN||01-06-2000||02:11 AM||||Here's what you do to fry your ECM!!!<P>1.  Get in a REAL BIG hurry!<BR>2.  Get your car all put together!<BR>3.  Have a massive Brain-fart!<BR>4.  Hook your battery cables up backwards!<P>Guaranteed to not only cook your ECM, but will also turn your alternator to toast!  I guess I had a little problem with that whole + & - thing.||||reg||9
